---
import { breadcrumbs, canonicalUrl } from '../../../lib/seo.js';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const path = '/guides/mcp-vs-api/';
const headline = "MCP vs API: what's the difference?";
---
<BaseLayout canonicalPath={path} type="article" structuredData={({ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@graph': [{ '@type': 'TechArticle', headline, url: canonicalUrl(path), publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'FreeMCP', url: canonicalUrl('/') }, mainEntityOfPage: canonicalUrl(path) }, breadcrumbs([['FreeMCP', '/'], ['MCP learning guides', '/guides/'], [headline, path]])] })} title="MCP vs API: what's the difference? — FreeMCP" description="Compare MCP and traditional APIs, and learn when developers use each.">
<article class="container info-page"><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/guides/">MCP learning guides</a></nav><div class="eyebrow">FREEMCP / LEARN</div><h1>MCP vs API: what's the difference?</h1><p class="intro">Compare MCP and traditional APIs, and learn when developers use each.</p><div class="info-content"><h2>They solve different problems</h2><p>An <strong>API</strong> defines how software systems exchange data or invoke operations. <strong>MCP</strong> defines a standard interaction model for AI applications to discover and use capabilities exposed by MCP servers.</p><h2>How MCP relates to APIs</h2><p>An MCP server often wraps one or more existing APIs. For example, a server might expose a search tool to an AI assistant while calling a conventional HTTP API behind the scenes.</p><h2>Key differences</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Traditional API</th><th>MCP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary audience</td><td>Software integrations</td><td>AI hosts and clients</td></tr><tr><td>Discovery</td><td>Varies by API and documentation</td><td>Standardized capability discovery</td></tr><tr><td>Capabilities</td><td>Endpoints and operations</td><td>Tools, resources, and prompts</td></tr><tr><td>Replacement?</td><td>Often powers the backend</td><td>Often integrates with APIs</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Which should you use?</h2><p>Use an API when building a direct software integration. Consider MCP when you want compatible AI applications to discover and interact with tools or context through a common protocol. Many systems use both.</p><p>Explore <a href="/guides/what-is-mcp/">the MCP basics</a> or <a href="/servers/">browse MCP servers</a>.</p></div></article>
</BaseLayout>
